Wrapping Up
    In this lesson, you’ve learned that each value of a particular type is called an instance (or an object).

    You’ve also learned that types aren't just labels like str or int, but that each type provides additional capabilities to its objects.

        Types have attributes (properties), which allow each instance to hold its own set of values.

        Types have instance methods, which are functions defined as part of the type that perform actions on that instance.

    Discovering Capabilities
        In Python, you can find out about the methods and attributes available without needing a heavy external browser. 
        You can access documentation directly from your code:

            Using dir(): Lists all the methods and attributes an object has.

            Using help(): Provides the "instruction manual" for a specific type or method.

            Editor Features: Most Python editors (like VS Code or PyCharm) will show you a "docstring" (a quick description) when you hover over a method or type your dot (.).

    You’ve seen that even a seemingly simple type like str has a large number of built-in methods ready for you to use.

    Why It Matters
        You’ve learned why it makes sense to provide functionality as instance methods and attributes rather than top-level functions. 
        It organizes your code, makes documentation easier to find, and allows your tools to provide better autocompletion.
    
    By bundling data and behavior into an object, Python hides the complex "how-to" and lets you focus on what you want to achieve.